{"name":"Candle Yellow","hex":"#e09b6e","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"candle","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A light source consisting of a wick embedded in a solid, flammable substance such as wax, tallow, or paraffin.","The protruding, removable portion of a filter, particularly a water filter."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To observe the growth of an embryo inside (an egg), using a bright light source.","To dry (greenware) prior to the firing cycle, setting the kiln at 200° Celsius until all water is removed from the greenware."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/candle"},{"term":"yellow","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of sunflower petals and lemons; the color obtained by mixing green and red light, or by subtracting blue from white light; the color evoked by light of wavelength around 580 nm; one of the three primary colors in subtractive color systems.","The middle light in a set of three traffic lights, the lighting of which indicates that drivers should stop short of the intersection if it is safe to do so."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a yellow hue.","Lacking courage."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/yellow"}],"entries":[{"title":"Candle","blocks":[{"text":"A candle is an ignitable wick embedded in wax, or another flammable solid substance such as tallow, that provides light, and in some cases, a fragrance. A candle can also provide heat or a method of keeping time. Candles have been used for over two millennia around the world, and were a significant form of indoor lighting until the invention of other types of light sources. Although electric light has largely made candle use nonessential for illumination, candles are still commonly used for functional, symbolic and aesthetic purposes and in specific cultural and religious settings."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/aa/LA2_Skultuna_kontorsljusstake.jpg/330px-LA2_Skultuna_kontorsljusstake.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Candle"},{"title":"Yellow","blocks":[{"text":"Yellow is a color between green and orange on the spectrum of light. It is evoked by light with a dominant wavelength of roughly 575–585 nm. It is a primary color in subtractive color systems, used in painting or color printing. In the RGB color model, used to create colors on television and computer screens, yellow is a secondary color made by combining red and green at equal intensity. Carotenoids give the characteristic yellow color to autumn leaves, corn, canaries, daffodils, and lemons, as well as egg yolks, buttercups, and bananas. They absorb light energy and protect plants from photo damage in some cases. Sunlight has a slight yellowish hue when the Sun is near the horizon, due to atmospheric scattering of shorter wavelengths."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d5/Cornwall_Daffodils.jpg/330px-Cornwall_Daffodils.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yellow"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/73/Always.gif/330px-Always.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/73/Always.gif/330px-Always.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The wick of a burning candle ","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAlways.gif"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5f/ISR-2013-Jerusalem-Church_of_the_Holy_Sepulchre-Candles.jpg/500px-ISR-2013-Jerusalem-Church_of_the_Holy_Sepulchre-Candles.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5f/ISR-2013-Jerusalem-Church_of_the_Holy_Sepulchre-Candles.jpg/1280px-ISR-2013-Jerusalem-Church_of_the_Holy_Sepulchre-Candles.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5f/ISR-2013-Jerusalem-Church_of_the_Holy_Sepulchre-Candles.jpg/500px-ISR-2013-Jerusalem-Church_of_the_Holy_Sepulchre-Candles.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Tapers (long thin candles) in a church","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AISR-2013-Jerusalem-Church_of_the_Holy_Sepulchre-Candles.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e0/Bronze_candelabrum_MET_DP250501.jpg/500px-Bronze_candelabrum_MET_DP250501.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e0/Bronze_candelabrum_MET_DP250501.jpg/1280px-Bronze_candelabrum_MET_DP250501.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e0/Bronze_candelabrum_MET_DP250501.jpg/500px-Bronze_candelabrum_MET_DP250501.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Etruscan candelabrum c. 550 BC","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABronze_candelabrum_MET_DP250501.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/56/%D0%A4%D0%B5%D0%BE%D1%84%D0%B8%D0%BB_III.jpg/500px-%D0%A4%D0%B5%D0%BE%D1%84%D0%B8%D0%BB_III.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/56/%D0%A4%D0%B5%D0%BE%D1%84%D0%B8%D0%BB_III.jpg/1280px-%D0%A4%D0%B5%D0%BE%D1%84%D0%B8%D0%BB_III.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/56/%D0%A4%D0%B5%D0%BE%D1%84%D0%B8%D0%BB_III.jpg/500px-%D0%A4%D0%B5%D0%BE%D1%84%D0%B8%D0%BB_III.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thu